<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zaire</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1971">1971</a>–<a href="/years/1997">1997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zaire was the official name of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> from 1971 to 1997, adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mobutu-sese-seko">Mobutu Sese Seko</a> as part of his campaign of 'authenticity'. Under Mobutu's one-party rule the country, rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, cobalt and diamonds, suffered from corruption and economic decline. In 1996–1997 an invasion by rebels under Laurent-Désiré Kabila, backed by Rwanda and Uganda, overthrew Mobutu, and the country resumed the name Democratic Republic of the Congo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zaire.jpg" alt="Zaire" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moyogo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Zaire_(1971%E2%80%931997).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Zaire was the Congolese state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-africa">Central Africa</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mobutu-sese-seko">Mobutu Sese Seko</a> headed from 1965 to 1997. In 1965 the division of power between president and parliament in Congo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Léopoldville</a>, a former Belgian colony, led to a stalemate, as it had in 1960. Joseph-Désiré Mobutu again seized power, but this time he took the presidency himself, following five years of upheaval after independence known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-crisis">Congo Crisis</a>. The country was officially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> until 1971. On 27 October 1971 he announced its renaming as the Republic of Zaire.</p><p>The name derives from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>, sometimes called Zaire in Portuguese, which in turn comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongo-language">Kikongo</a> nzere o zadi, the river that swallows rivers. Under the policy of authenticité Zairians were required to adopt African names, and Mobutu himself became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mobutu-sese-seko">Mobutu Sese Seko</a>. Cities were renamed too, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinshasa">Léopoldville</a> became Kinshasa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kisangani">Stanleyville</a> became Kisangani.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By 1967 Mobutu had given the country a new constitution, approved by 98 per cent of those voting in a referendum in June, which centralised executive power in the president. On 17 April 1967 he created the Popular Movement of the Revolution, the MPR, as the only party. In the presidential election of 1 November 1970 only the MPR could nominate a candidate, and Mobutu was confirmed with 10,131,000 votes against 157.</p><p>They were driven out with Western military help. By 1979 purchasing power was only 4 per cent of its level in 1960, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">IMF</a> loans from 1976 were largely embezzled. After the end of the Cold War Mobutu had to declare a new republic in 1990 and allow a multi-party system. In 1996 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> militias that had fled Rwanda, allied with the Zairian army, attacked Congolese Tutsis in the east, and the Tutsi militias rose in the First Congo War. Laurent-Désiré Kabila led the rebellion, Mobutu fled, and the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> was restored in 1997. Mobutu died less than four months later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Zaire was a unitary presidential state in which the president appointed and dismissed ministers, provincial governors and judges. Its doctrine, Mobutism, was set out in the Manifesto of N'sele of May 1967, which named nationalism, revolution and authenticity as its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> and declared the regime neither right nor left. Authenticity also led Zairians to abandon Western dress for the abacost. The currency was the zaïre, divided into 100 makuta.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zaire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…overthrew the elected government of Salvador Allende, and Argentina in 1976, and repeatedly in Nigeria, Ghana and many other African states; independence leaders banned rival parties; and personal rulers such as Mobutu Sese Seko in <b>Zaire</b> (1965–1997) and Ferdinand Marcos in the Philippines (1965–1986) held power for decades.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Party membership is usually a minority of the population, though Mobutu Sese Seko’s <b>Zaire</b> enrolled every citizen in its ruling movement.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · How It Works</a></li></ul></section>
